# Copy to .env for local dev, and set the same keys in
# Vercel → Project Settings → Environment Variables for production.

# Required server-side secrets (never exposed to the browser)
RSE_API_KEY=
ROBOTEVENTS_API_KEY=

# Optional upstream base overrides (defaults shown)
# ROBOSTEM_API_BASE=https://robostem-api.org
# ROBOTEVENTS_API_BASE=https://events.vex.com/api/v2
# ROBOTEVENTS_ROOT_BASE=https://events.vex.com
# JUMPER_API_BASE=https://jumper.robostem.org

# ── Proxy abuse protection (server-side; both optional) ───────────────────────
# The /api proxy attaches the secret keys above to every request, so without a
# guard anyone who finds the deployed URL can drive it as a free relay to burn
# your upstream quota (the key value itself is never exposed to the browser).
#
# APP_ORIGIN: comma-separated allowlist of full origins permitted to use the
# proxy. When UNSET, origin enforcement is DISABLED (so a missing var can't take
# prod down) and only the rate limit applies. localhost:5173/5175 are always ok.
#   APP_ORIGIN=https://openscout.ing,https://www.openscout.ing
#
# Upstash Redis for rate limiting (60 req / 10s). When either is unset the
# limiter is a no-op (e.g. local dev needs no setup). Create a free DB at
# console.upstash.com and copy the REST URL + token.
#
# The bucket key is the authenticated user id when a request carries a valid
# bearer token, and the client IP otherwise — a whole school shares one NAT
# address, so per-IP alone would throttle a club as if it were one bad actor.
#   UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
#   UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# ── Native-client auth (the iOS app) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# A browser always sends Origin or Referer, so APP_ORIGIN is enough to authorize
# it. URLSession sends neither, so the iOS app would 403 on every proxied call.
# Instead it presents a Supabase access token, which the proxy verifies before
# allowing the request (see server/appAuth.ts).
#
# Verification prefers the project's public JWKS and needs no secret — it is
# derived from SUPABASE_URL (falling back to VITE_SUPABASE_URL), so if that is
# already set there is nothing more to configure.
#
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET is only for projects still issuing legacy HS256 tokens.
# Find it under Settings → API → JWT Settings. It is a genuine secret: anyone
# holding it can mint a token for any user. Never expose it to the browser.
#   SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=
#
# Also required, in the Supabase dashboard rather than here: enable
# Authentication → Providers → Anonymous sign-in. Without it the app cannot get
# a token before the user signs in, and browsing would require an account.

# YouTube Data API v3 key for the Jumper tab. Browser-exposed by design
# (VITE_ prefix) — restrict it to the YouTube Data API + your domains in the
# Google Cloud console.
VITE_DEFAULT_YOUTUBE_API_KEY=

# Supabase (accounts). The publishable key (`sb_publishable_…`) is public by
# design — it's safe in the browser because access is enforced by Row Level
# Security. Use the SAME values locally and on Vercel so sessions and synced data
# match across environments.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# ── Google sign-in branding (VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID) ───────────────────────────
# signInWithOAuth sends the browser to Supabase's own /auth/v1/callback, so
# Google's account chooser reads "Sign in to <project-ref>.supabase.co". The
# only way to change that host is Supabase's paid custom-domain add-on.
#
# Instead the button runs the OpenID Connect implicit flow against Google
# directly with redirect_uri on our own domain (src/google-signin.ts), which is
# the host the chooser names. Set this to the SAME OAuth 2.0 Web client ID
# already listed in the Supabase dashboard → Authentication → Google → Client
# IDs, or the ID token exchange is rejected. Public by design — it's in the
# token's `aud` claim.
#
# In the Google Cloud console that client needs, under "Authorized redirect
# URIs", the bare origin of every URL the app runs on — no trailing slash, which
# the console strips anyway, and Google matches the string exactly:
#   https://openscout.ing   https://forge.robostem.org   http://localhost:5175
# Keep the existing <project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback entry too —
# Discord and the fallback path still use it. Vercel preview URLs can't be
# registered (they're random), so previews fall back to the old redirect flow.
#
# When this is unset the button silently falls back to that old flow,
# supabase.co branding and all.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# ── Event grade levels (/api/event-grades) ────────────────────────────────────
# RobotEvents publishes an event's grade level ONLY as text on the public event
# page, which sits behind Cloudflare — so the page is fetched through cors.sh.
# Keyless requests work at a lower limit; a key raises the monthly quota. Get one
# at cors.sh. Server-side only.
#   CORS_SH_API_KEY=
#
# A cors.sh live key 403s ("Live key requires a browser Origin") unless the
# request carries an Origin header. We send it server-side, so it only has to be
# ONE valid origin — it does not have to match where the code runs, which is why
# local dev works with the production origin. By default we send the first entry
# of APP_ORIGIN (which is a comma-separated allowlist and so can't be sent as-is)
# and fall back to https://openscout.ing. Override it explicitly here:
#   CORS_SH_ORIGIN=https://openscout.ing
#
# Grade level never changes after an event is created, so every successful scrape
# is written to the `event_grades` table (migration 0009) and never fetched again.
# That write needs the SECRET key (`sb_secret_…`), which bypasses RLS — keep it
# server-side only (no VITE_ prefix) and never commit it. Without it the lookup
# still works, it just re-scrapes and burns quota (the server logs a warning).
# READS fall back to the publishable key, since `event_grades` is public-select.
# SUPABASE_URL defaults to VITE_SUPABASE_URL.
#   SUPABASE_URL=
#   SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# ── Supabase Auth URL configuration (NOT an env var — set in the project) ──────
# OAuth sign-in redirects are governed by the Supabase project's auth config, not
# by anything in this repo. The app sends redirectTo: window.location.origin (and
# window.location.href for /join/<token> deep links), but Supabase only honors a
# redirect if it matches the allow-list; otherwise it falls back to Site URL.
#
# If post-login lands on localhost in production, the Site URL is still dev. Fix:
#   Dashboard → Authentication → URL Configuration, or via the Management API:
#     site_url      = https://openscout.ing
#     uri_allow_list = https://openscout.ing/**,https://www.openscout.ing/**,https://forge.robostem.org/**,https://*-axcdengs-projects.vercel.app/**
#   (the /** wildcard is required so /join/<token> deep links are honored. www is
#    kept in the allow-list as a fallback, but apex is the canonical Site URL.)
#
# Do NOT use `supabase config push` for this — it treats config.toml as the full
# source of truth and would disable the Discord/Google OAuth providers. PATCH the
# two fields surgically instead:
#   curl -X PATCH -H "Authorization: Bearer $(security find-generic-password -s 'Supabase CLI' -w)" \
#     -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
#     -d '{"site_url":"https://openscout.ing","uri_allow_list":"https://openscout.ing/**,https://www.openscout.ing/**,https://forge.robostem.org/**,https://*-axcdengs-projects.vercel.app/**"}' \
#     https://api.supabase.com/v1/projects/<project-ref>/config/auth

# ── Translation (/api/translate) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Non-English event/team/org names are translated to English for display. Chrome
# does it on-device for free; every other browser calls MyMemory's free public
# API through this endpoint. Anonymous callers get 5,000 words/day per IP.
# Setting an email address raises that to 50,000/day — no account and no card,
# the address is just an identifier the API attributes usage to. Server-side.
#   MYMEMORY_EMAIL=
#
# Every successful translation is written to the `translations` table (migration
# 0010) and never fetched again, so the quota is a one-time cost per unique
# string across all users. That write needs SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY (same key as the
# event-grades cache); without it lookups still work, they just re-translate.
